\index.html This page is the landing page. It will detect from browser storage if the operator has a saved session or not. If there is a save session log the user into bunker.html forcing an authentication on the client side.
\forge.html This page will assist the new operator in 'forging' a new identity. It will creat a new kaspa wallet with 24 word passphrase. Also handles the rest of first-time onboarding in one continuous flow (merged from the former initialize.html): once a password is set, it automatically requests starter faucet grants, broadcasts the Genesis transaction (1,000 $PUNKW), and funds the Gameplay Vault via a real Buy Turns purchase, before landing the operator in bunker.html.
\import.html This page will assist an operator to access there identity by entering a 24 word passphrase
\resume.html This page will assist an operator who has their identity stored in their browser cache to reauthenticate and enter the bunker
\bunker.html This page will support the primary game play loop. Has the operators meta information and log of recent activities. In the bunker the operator can Phish to general more $PUNKW without navigating to another screen. Clicking the Phish button expends one cycle and generates a random amount of $PUNKW based on a formula which includes the number of sectors they have and the highest level hack they have researched.
\leaderboard.html This page will list (wallets) operators with a CPW memo transaction, ranking them by various facets of the game metrics. Sectors, PUNKW, Total Unit Strength
\send.html Wallet Management -- general Plain Wallet sends, a demo of the Gameplay Vault covenant rejecting a non-CPW spend, and Buy Turns (fund the Gameplay Vault from the Plain Wallet, paying the faucet/host cut).
\history.html CPW History -- decodes an address's on-chain CPW1 payloads into plain-language moves (e.g. "Executed Phishing Attack -- Earned 47 $PUNKW"). Defaults to the operator's own Plain Wallet + Gameplay Vault merged; can also look up any other address. Combines the REST indexer's transaction list (payload-less on this deployment) with best-effort direct-RPC payload decoding (works until a node prunes the block) -- see kaspa-client.js's fetchAddressTransactionList/fetchTransactionPayloadBytes.
\attack.html This page supports the operator in initializing an attack on another operator. Currently unbuilt (COMING_SOON) -- its entry point now lives as a card in bunker.html's Command Center action grid rather than its own persistent-nav destination; this page is an unlinked placeholder until the real interaction flow is built.
\research.html This page supports the operator in investing cycles into discovery new hacks. Currently unbuilt (COMING_SOON) -- same nav treatment as attack.html above.
\hack.html This page supports casting hacks previously researched. The most important of which is armageddon which if successfully cast resets the season.
\gameplay.html help page that explains the game. It defines terms such as Cycles, Hacks, Sectors, $PUNKW. It explains the overall seasonal meta (seasons end when the armageddon ddos is initiated by an operator and has run to completion without interuption). Etc.. Not pertinent to actually executing gameplay -- lives flat alongside every other page (not in a subfolder), same as the rest of this list.
\architecture.html help page that explains how the game is constructed as an html web site, integrated with Kaspa using Kaspa-WASM. How the data is stored in the DAG (a tagged `payload` field, not a Bitcoin-style OP_RETURN). How the game logic is verified decentralized.
\whitepaper.html help page explaining the Kaspathon genesis, the nod to Archmage, and how rebuilding Archmage as a decentralized project became a fascinating way to showcase the ideas for vprogs (a real but not-yet-live Kaspa roadmap concept -- this game's tagged-payload/covenant approach is a working proof of concept for that direction, not vprogs itself).
\design-bible.md developer-facing (not player-facing) reference mapping legacy Archmage/The Reincarnation mechanics onto CPW systems -- Research trees, unit/army composition, payload/spell design, item/hardware design, infrastructure economy. Lives at the repo root since it's a build doc, not in-fiction lore.
